  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSurface anticyclone has kept weather conditions mild across the area \ntoday, with WAA at 850mb helping to boost surface temperatures into \nthe lower 50s. A few passing high-level clouds have been spotted due \nto advection from cloud cover present over parts of the Central \nPlains. The mid-level ridge axis will be eastward of the forecast \narea by Saturday morning, with 850mb thermal ridge passing through. \nThis will bring Saturday afternoon high temperatures into mid 50s \nfor most of the area, with lower 50s in northern Missouri. \n\nMid and uppper-level pattern upstream will remain active with strong \ncyclonic vorticity and increased flow. This dCVA will start to head \neastward into the Central Plains, with mid-level height falls \nthroughout the afternoon and surface cyclogenesis over the Front \nRange. This will develop a panhandle hook cyclone. Stronger moisture \nadvection is expected with this system late Saturday as it starts to \nlift northeastward out of the Panhandle Region, and moisture \nwrapping around to the back side. Ensemble probabilities for much of \nthe area seeing rain late Saturday through Sunday are over 70 \npercent for most of the area, with portions north of Interstate 70 \nover 90 percent. Although on the cold side of the cyclone, forecast \ntemperatures remain above freezing during the time of forcing for \nprecipitation, therefore, this is forecast to remain a rain event. \nThe greater instability gets pushed southeast of the forecast into \nthe open warm-sector, therefore, the forecast will not provide any \nmention of thunderstorm potential with this system. Locally heavy-\nrainfall may become possible, especially if we get a strong warm-\nconveyor belt that brings plenty of moisture, and could have some \nFGEN processes within a deformation zone. Sunday afternoon, strong \ncold front moves through, that will bring temperatures back down \ninto the 40s. \n\nNext week, deep trough is progged over the western CONUS that will \nforce an amplified ridge east of the Rockies, providing stronger WAA \nthat bring above normal temperatures for the first week of December, \nwith lower 60s possible by Wednesday of next week. Eventually this \nstronger flow arrives in the central CONUS by the end of the next \nworkweek that will likely bring some rain activity along with more \nseasonable temperatures heading into the following weekend.",
